## Looking is advisory, measuring is not

A render tells you whether the object is the RIGHT object. It cannot
tell you whether the mesh is sound. Disconnected shells, geometry
passing through geometry and inward normals are all invisible in a
picture and all fatal downstream. Never let a good-looking render
substitute for the gate.

The bias runs one way, and it is measured. Visual verifiers detect a
correct edit with high recall and low precision — they say "looks fine"
when it is not, far more often than the reverse. Their agreement with
human judgment also DEGRADES as the thing being judged gets better:
correlation near 0.77 on crude output, near 0.46 on good output. The
closer you are to done, the less the eye is worth.

So a favourable verdict is weak evidence and an unfavourable one is
strong. Treat "no deviation" as "nothing was caught," never as "nothing
is wrong."

## Getting a usable image

- Assert the camera before you trust the frame. A view you believe is
  three-quarter and is actually front will hide exactly the defect the
  angle existed to reveal.
- One angle answers one question. A part can be missing, offset or
  intersecting and be perfectly hidden from a single side.
- If a view cannot answer the question, say so and take another. A
  confident reading of an image that does not show the part is worse
  than no reading.

## After a fix

Re-render and check the specific item you were told about, by name,
before you report it fixed. A coding agent's fix does not always land,
and the failure is silent: the critique is answered in prose, the
geometry is unchanged, and the next round starts from a false premise.
"Did the edit land" is a different question from "is it correct now,"
and it is the cheaper of the two.
